WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:03.837
The general feeling amongst physicists,
for the last hundred years

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:07.966
is that we need to quantize space time
just as we quantized every other field.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:11.511
But reconciling gravity with quantum
theory

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.514
is one of the great challenges of physics.

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:16.766
Thus far
we've been hopelessly unsuccessful.

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:19.561
And so think, we're at a point
where we ought to really question

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:22.564
whether this drive
and this challenge to quantize gravity,

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:25.984
whether that really
was the right thing to do

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:30.321
My name is Jonathan Oppenheim.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:33.950
I'm a physicist at University
College London. And myself

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.911
and my students have proposed
a theory of gravity

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.622
where matter remains quantum,

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:42.625
but somehow gravity is treated
classically.

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:46.546
It's an attempt to reconcile the fact
that our current theory of gravity,

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.966
which is general relativity,
is inconsistent with quantum mechanics.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:55.138
In physics we have these two frameworks,
quantum mechanics and classical mechanics,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.768
all the particles and all the fields
and different forces and electromagnetism

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:01.019
and all that we know in nature

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:04.814
that we've discovered,
they all obey quantum theory.

00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:07.734
But quantum theory doesn't
describe gravity.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.945
Our theory of gravity is general
relativity discovered by Einstein

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:13.156
And it is a theory of spacetime.

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:17.202
So it tells us that spacetime bends
and it is not a quantum theory at all.

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:19.954
The general feeling amongst physicists
for the last hundred years

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:22.499
is that gravity is a force
just like any other force,

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.418
and therefore it should be quantized,
just like every other force.

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:27.837
And, fit into the framework of quantum
theory.

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:29.756
But gravity is different.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:32.634
gravity is very simple.

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:35.261
In some ways,
almost all of gravity is just that.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:38.264
Clocks run at different
speeds in different places.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:43.353
And that amazingly gives you
the curvature of space time

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:48.691
gravity is very much about the arena
or the background in which matter moves.

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:53.988
So you have this quantized matter
which evolves in time and moves in space.

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:58.284
and this background space time
appears to be a necessary part

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.287
of how we describe quantum theory.

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:06.584
So let's say you believe
that we should quantize space-time

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:09.879
what we're doing is we're quantizing
how fast clocks run.

00:02:10.338 --> 00:02:13.508
And it turns out if you try and quantize

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:16.594
that, you're essentially quantizing
 the speed of time.

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:20.974
And if you quantize the speed of time,
what you find is that time becomes frozen

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.228
things don't evolve at all.

00:02:25.228 --> 00:02:28.064
So this is the immediate problem
that people grappled with

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:32.152
when they were trying to quantized gravity
and we've been stuck on that ever since.

00:02:33.903 --> 00:02:37.866
the black hole information paradox
or annoyance or problem,

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.326
however you want to describe it,
is probably

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.288
one of the most fascinating conundrums
we have in physics.

00:02:43.288 --> 00:02:46.916
it somehow seems to hold the key
to the way to reconcile

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.919
gravity and quantum theory.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.548
This problem
which comes about because of Hawking's

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:57.343
discovery that black holes
appear to thermally radiate.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:02.724
So if you throw known a quantum state,
a known item into a black hole,

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:06.477
then the black hole will just radiate
in a way thermally, in a way that seems

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:09.647
to completely destroy the information
that was thrown into the black hole.

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:12.775
If you believe in a fully quantum
theory of gravity, you're pretty well

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:16.404
compelled to believe that information
has to come out of a black hole.

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:18.907
And therefore,
you have a bit of a paradox,

00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:21.618
But It's only a paradox
if you believe that physics

00:03:21.618 --> 00:03:25.205
somehow has to be deterministic, predicting
everything with certainty.

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:29.667
It's not a paradox if you don't believe
that physics is deterministic,

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:34.172
if you believe that there can be noise
and stochasticity and randomness,

00:03:34.297 --> 00:03:37.217
if you believe that,
then it's not really a paradox at all.

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.636
You throw in a known state
into the black hole

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:42.722
and it comes out random,
and that's just the way life is.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:43.348
And I think

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:47.185
a lot of phyisicists are very uncomfortable
if there's a fundamental stochasticity,

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:47.810
or randomness.

00:03:49.312 --> 00:03:51.648
Kg squared

00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:54.651
6 seconds meters to the minus.

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:55.485
The

00:03:55.485 --> 00:03:58.571
idea that we can consistently
couple quantum systems

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:00.865
and classical systems has been around
since the nineties

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:02.492
and it was exciting to me

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:05.495
that these two frameworks
somehow can be made to fit together.

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:09.415
if space time is fundamentally
the classical background in which matter

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.543
moves, then things become easier
in many different ways.

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:16.673
So, for example,
you now can have an evolving spacetime.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:20.635
The black hole information paradox
no longer is a paradox.

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:24.639
Information can be destroyed
if the background spacetime is classical.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:28.601
And so what we, you know, myself
or my students were able to prove

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:32.939
is that any consistent theory of coupling
between classical degrees of freedom

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:36.943
and quantum degrees of freedom
have to be fundamentally stochastic.

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:40.655
Any theory in which gravity
is fundamentally classical

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:42.407
has to have this randomness.

00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:46.035
And the amount of randomness
you have to have is a lot.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.622
Ten to the minus one upper bound.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:52.625
and then the lower bound
is ten to the -40.

00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:56.546
So a few years ago, after
I'd given a talk on the theory,

00:04:56.587 --> 00:05:00.133
I got into a discussion with Carlo
Rovelli, who's one of the founders of

00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:01.259
loop quantum gravity.

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:05.221
And as a result of that discussion,
we formulated a bet with 5000 to 1 odds

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:08.891
on whether spacetime
is fundamentally quantum or not.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:11.894
Geoff Pennington, who's a leading string
theorist, also joined in on the BET.

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:15.064
if I win,
I will get 5000 items from each of them,

00:05:15.106 --> 00:05:19.610
maybe some olive oil or a little dram
of whiskey maybe, a packet of crisps

00:05:19.944 --> 00:05:23.781
And so what I like about the bets
is that they force us to,

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.908
first of all,
make the bet precise in a way

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:27.785
that can be tested experimentally,
because I think

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:30.788
that's important that we can test
our beliefs experimentally.

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:33.541
we have these experiments
that we're proposing

00:05:33.541 --> 00:05:36.544
looking for randomness
in the gravitational field, which are,

00:05:36.794 --> 00:05:39.839
I would say, an indirect test
of a quantum theory of gravity in that

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:42.675
they're maybe more of a test
of a classical theory of gravity.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:46.012
But of
course, if you don't find randomness

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:49.390
and noise in the gravitational field,
then you can rule out the classical theory

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:50.183
of gravity.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:50.600
Blah, blah, blah,

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.645
So we measure the gravitational field,
say, produced by a one kilogram mass,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:59.025
and we can measure how much noise
there is in a precision test of gravity.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:03.363
And then we can go out and we can measure

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:06.366
how long we can put
a massive object in superposition.

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:11.329
So you imagine a gold atom, for example,
And those two experiments squeeze

00:06:11.329 --> 00:06:13.456
any theory in which gravity is treated
fundamentally.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:16.459
Classically,
it squeezes it from two directions,

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:18.878
We're all really struggling
to solve this problem.

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.214
We've been struggling for a hundred years.

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:25.218
And so the those experiments
will determine the outcome of the bet.

